WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.933
Steve: Good morning or good afternoon, I don't know.

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:04.967
Laura: Good afternoon.

00:00:05.933 --> 00:00:13.000
Steve: I have the great pleasure
to be able to speak with a Spanish polyglot.

00:00:13.001 --> 00:00:15.201
Who lives in Germany,

00:00:15.327 --> 00:00:19.600
who teaches languages through
the internet and whose name is Laura.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.333
Native of Barcelona.

00:00:22.333 --> 00:00:25.233
Good afternoon Laura.

00:00:25.233 --> 00:00:27.800
Laura: Good afternoon Steve.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:29.467
The honor is mine, in fact.

00:00:29.467 --> 00:00:32.767
I'm delighted to speak with you
and have the chance to meet you.

00:00:32.767 --> 00:00:36.500
I love your videos,
so it's amazing to get to know you.

00:00:36.501 --> 00:00:37.733
Thank you so much.

00:00:37.734 --> 00:00:42.833
Steve: Too much... How do you say?

00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:46.233
Polite.

00:00:46.234 --> 00:00:48.067
How do you say "polite" in spanish?

00:00:48.067 --> 00:00:51.867
Laura: Ah ehmm...yes.
Too much...

00:00:51.867 --> 00:00:53.167
Oh, see?
That´s what happens to me.

00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:56.900
As a polyglot, we are a bit
bilingual and forget the words.

00:00:56.901 --> 00:01:01.100
Steve: Kind?
Well, let's go with kind.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.000
Laura: Yes, well educated.
Steve: Well educated?

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:03.933
But hey, kind.

00:01:03.933 --> 00:01:08.567
Laura: It's the truth Steve,
because I watch your videos

00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:11.100
And man, as a polyglot, it's normal,
isn't it?

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.867
You inspire us all.

00:01:12.867 --> 00:01:15.867
Well, I think I speak
for other people as well.

00:01:15.867 --> 00:01:18.100
Because yes, you're an inspiration.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.433
And I'm looking forward to talking to you.

00:01:20.433 --> 00:01:21.533
Steve: Well, thanks.

00:01:21.533 --> 00:01:26.500
If you viewers enjoy my videos,

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:31.400
you can click the bell
to subscribe to updates.

00:01:36.633 --> 00:01:42.267
One thing that came to my mind is that

00:01:42.267 --> 00:01:46.933
though it's not my strongest language,

00:01:46.933 --> 00:01:49.667
I can still use Spanish.

00:01:49.667 --> 00:01:52.767
However, there are always words,

00:01:52.767 --> 00:01:55.967
that you forget, that you don't know,

00:01:55.967 --> 00:02:01.433
that, at the time you want to use them,
are not searchable.

00:02:01.433 --> 00:02:04.367
For example "polite".
How do you say polite?

00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:06.933
There are many examples.

00:02:06.933 --> 00:02:10.533
Now I am mixing
Spanish and Portuguese, mostly.

00:02:11.433 --> 00:02:17.133
I think that many times,
people who study languages

00:02:17.133 --> 00:02:20.133
feel the frustration

00:02:20.133 --> 00:02:26.133
of not being able to look up the word

00:02:26.133 --> 00:02:29.033
at the time they want to use it.

00:02:29.033 --> 00:02:32.767
And this is of no importance,

00:02:32.767 --> 00:02:40.300
just the fact that it doesn't feel so good.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.300
Which seems worse.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:46.433
It's a matter of pride.

00:02:46.433 --> 00:02:48.833
But it has no significance.

00:02:51.733 --> 00:02:51.813
It's not that important.

00:02:53.433 --> 00:02:54.733
Laura: Exactly, absolutely.

00:02:56.033 --> 00:02:59.033
That happens to me a lot,
because look, I live in Germany.

00:02:59.033 --> 00:03:01.233
And since I have learned so many languages,

00:03:01.233 --> 00:03:04.367
and I've been living in Germany for 10 years;
and my daily life is in German.

00:03:04.367 --> 00:03:08.067
My mother tongues
are Catalan and Spanish,

00:03:08.067 --> 00:03:11.233
but I even forget my own mother tongues.

00:03:11.233 --> 00:03:12.867
Sometimes I'm talking to my parents

00:03:12.867 --> 00:03:14.767
and I say "How did you say that?"

00:03:14.767 --> 00:03:17.900
I therefore constantly say
that this is common.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:20.600
For example in my channel,
which is called "Couch polyglot",

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:24.567
what I am trying to convey is
that making mistakes is normal.

00:03:24.567 --> 00:03:29.067
That it is human, and that learning
languages has to be for fun.

00:03:29.067 --> 00:03:31.500
That has to be the goal.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.033
And do not become obsessed.

00:03:33.033 --> 00:03:36.133
"I misspoke this word" or
"How do you pronounce that?"

00:03:36.133 --> 00:03:37.733
Well, you can rephrase it.

00:03:37.733 --> 00:03:40.267
You create the sentence
in a different way and that's it.

00:03:41.033 --> 00:03:42.000
Steve: Exactly.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.167
One thing, though...

00:03:43.167 --> 00:03:46.067
In the case of the Ukrainian...

00:03:46.067 --> 00:03:52.800
because in Ukraine, people speak
half usually in Russian,

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:56.167
and the other half usually in Ukrainian.

00:03:56.167 --> 00:04:02.700
But there is also a language
called "Súrzhik" in Ukraine.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:04.800
Which is a mix.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:10.133
Because people
are bound to get confused.

00:04:10.133 --> 00:04:15.933
They will use Russian words
in Ukrainian, and vice versa.

00:04:15.933 --> 00:04:19.467
This phenomenon is
often seen in Catalonia.

00:04:19.467 --> 00:04:23.900
Because the two languages
have a lot of each other.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:31.500
Can it happen that a person
think he/she speaks Spanish

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:33.867
but uses Catalan words?

00:04:33.867 --> 00:04:38.067
Or the other way around, speaking
Catalan to use Spanish words.

00:04:38.067 --> 00:04:40.967
Laura: Yes, it happens a lot.

00:04:40.967 --> 00:04:43.933
In fact even,
when I speak in Catalan,

00:04:43.933 --> 00:04:46.300
there are some "castellanismos",
which are called,

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:47.933
which are very much incorporated.

00:04:47.933 --> 00:04:52.133
For example, instead of
say "ve" we say "buenu".

00:04:52.133 --> 00:04:54.333
We "Catalanize" it a bit.

00:04:54.333 --> 00:04:56.733
We don't say "bueno", we say "buenu".

00:04:56.733 --> 00:04:58.667
Things like that, right?

00:04:58.667 --> 00:05:01.467
And that´s part of everyday life.

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:05.900
I try to avoid that in my videos,
but in real life people talk like that.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:08.833
Languages are a little bit
mingled in the street.

00:05:08.833 --> 00:05:13.200
Or occasionally you might be narrating
an anecdote in Catalan

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:16.733
when you accidentally slip in
a hilarious Spanish term.

00:05:16.733 --> 00:05:18.567
That might occur, for instance.

00:05:18.567 --> 00:05:20.067
And also the other way around.

00:05:20.067 --> 00:05:23.133
Those who mistakenly believe
a word to be Spanish

00:05:23.133 --> 00:05:25.100
when in fact it has Catalan roots.

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:29.500
I believe that this is inevitable,
because the languages are in contact.

00:05:30.267 --> 00:05:32.200
Steve: Yes, it is inevitable.
It's like this.

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:36.700
That languages will eventually evolve.

00:05:39.133 --> 00:05:44.333
In English, for example, there are
now few people using, for example,

00:05:44.333 --> 00:05:48.200
"Whom" "To whom?"
"Are you speaking?" "To whom?"

00:05:50.067 --> 00:05:54.600
I use it because I am old
and I still use "whom".

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:59.000
Now there are people who
say "I would have went".

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.467
Which is awful.

00:06:02.467 --> 00:06:06.000
It makes my ears hurt.

00:06:06.933 --> 00:06:13.400
But it is possible that in 50 years
it will be normal, won't it?

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:18.867
Because languages are always
changing and evolving.

00:06:18.867 --> 00:06:25.133
I hope that the Catalan
language survives.

00:06:25.133 --> 00:06:27.567
Laura: Oh, I hope so too.

00:06:27.567 --> 00:06:31.200
Honestly.
We´ll see.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:37.633
Steve: In your channel, which I will
leave a link in the description,

00:06:37.633 --> 00:06:40.467
and it's called "Couch polyglot", right?

00:06:40.467 --> 00:06:41.833
Laura: Yes.

00:06:41.833 --> 00:06:43.500
Steve: What is it all about?

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:45.400
What is the goal?

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:49.967
What do you want to achieve
with this platform?

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:53.333
Laura: Well look, that was
really spontaneous.

00:06:53.333 --> 00:06:56.367
It was early in the pandemic.

00:06:56.367 --> 00:06:58.800
And I am a very outgoing
person,

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:03.167
and I was climbing the walls from stress
and not knowing what was going to happen.

00:07:03.167 --> 00:07:06.833
And I said "Oh, I need a new hobby.
I'm going to create a YouTube channel.

00:07:06.833 --> 00:07:08.600
And it went like this.

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:13.267
And then I decided to create
videos in different languages.

00:07:13.267 --> 00:07:16.967
For example, I create videos
teaching Catalan, Spanish and German.

00:07:16.967 --> 00:07:19.400
Which are like my strongest tongues.

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:23.733
Then I also create videos
showing how I learn Italian,

00:07:23.733 --> 00:07:26.100
how i learn swedish,
by doing experiments,

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:28.633
doing interviews
with other polyglots.

00:07:28.633 --> 00:07:30.967
So it's a bit of a mixed bag.

00:07:30.967 --> 00:07:35.067
It is not just a language,
it is thus a potpourri.

00:07:35.067 --> 00:07:37.133
Steve: Yes, yes.
Very goog.

00:07:37.133 --> 00:07:41.433
And after this interview,
we are going to talk on your channel

00:07:41.433 --> 00:07:48.967
in different languages,
so we can show this topic.

00:07:48.967 --> 00:07:52.033
I think the fact of being able
to speak many languages

00:07:52.033 --> 00:07:54.233
5, 4, 6, 7...

00:07:54.233 --> 00:07:57.967
is within everyone's reach.

00:07:57.967 --> 00:08:03.633
People do not need talent,

00:08:03.633 --> 00:08:05.500
they need interest.

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:07.900
And the brain is very flexible.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:13.433
Maybe not all at once,
but one language after the other

00:08:13.433 --> 00:08:16.600
you could learn 4, 5, 6, languages.

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:18.033
And speak them quite well.

00:08:18.033 --> 00:08:20.567
When I say I speak 20 languages,

00:08:20.567 --> 00:08:23.200
I don't mean I speak 20 languages well.
No.

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:26.567
I speak five to six languages fluently.

00:08:26.567 --> 00:08:29.833
There are 5 others that I speak,
but not as well.

00:08:29.833 --> 00:08:31.233
And some with great difficulty.

00:08:31.233 --> 00:08:40.333
But I always have a great time
learning and exploring

00:08:40.333 --> 00:08:43.767
languages, cultures and all of that.

00:08:43.767 --> 00:08:46.600
And everyone can do that.

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:49.167
If you have the interest
and the motivation.

00:08:50.267 --> 00:08:52.300
Laura: Yes, I have
seen it for real.

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:55.667
Because one of my friends
struggled with English at school.

00:08:55.667 --> 00:09:00.933
It is also true that in school, english
is taught in a very theoretical way.

00:09:00.933 --> 00:09:04.167
Perhaps because it does not awaken
the interest of the students and so on.

00:09:04.167 --> 00:09:05.767
Well, maybe it has changed.

00:09:05.767 --> 00:09:09.900
But then, later on,
she fell in love with a French boy

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:11.667
and learned French very quickly.

00:09:11.667 --> 00:09:14.633
So there she had a very strong
motivation,

00:09:14.633 --> 00:09:17.400
and then at that moment
she realized

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:19.600
Ah, I'm capable of learning languages."

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:22.033
But sometimes motivation
is a little lacking.

00:09:22.033 --> 00:09:24.667
And there are many people
who have the belief of

00:09:24.667 --> 00:09:28.867
"Since I couldn't learn English
as a teenager, or as a child,

00:09:28.867 --> 00:09:33.000
is that I no longer have the talent
or I won't be able to.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:36.033
And we need to completely
alter that mindset.

00:09:36.033 --> 00:09:40.900
It's what you say, I think that
everyone can learn languages.

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:42.533
So do I, too.

00:09:42.533 --> 00:09:45.333
Steve: I believe that the fact
that in schools they teach

00:09:45.333 --> 00:09:51.567
grammar rules so people may think
that they need memory,

00:09:51.567 --> 00:09:57.433
or that they need an ability
to understand those rules.

00:09:57.433 --> 00:10:06.267
But in fact, if you don't have enough
experience with this language,

00:10:06.267 --> 00:10:09.267
all explanations are worthless.

00:10:09.267 --> 00:10:15.267
That's why I always say,
that they must allow people to

00:10:15.267 --> 00:10:17.967
study the language they are interested in.

00:10:17.967 --> 00:10:20.833
For example, in Canada,
all English speakers,

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:22.267
have to learn French.

00:10:22.267 --> 00:10:24.433
If someone does not want to
learn French,

00:10:24.433 --> 00:10:30.933
is not going to learn French in order to
to have a sense of patriotism.

00:10:30.933 --> 00:10:35.667
If someone wants to
learn Spanish or Chinese,

00:10:35.667 --> 00:10:39.400
let them learn
the language they like.

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:44.067
And then they can learn
French, whenever they want.

00:10:44.067 --> 00:10:48.300
So I believe that the way
of teaching languages in schools,

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:50.700
has done a lot of damage.

00:10:51.833 --> 00:10:59.000
People who are interested,
are interested in the same way.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:01.233
Laura: Anyway, yes.

00:11:01.233 --> 00:11:05.500
But I, for example, the first language
I learned, let's say consciously,

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:06.533
was English.

00:11:06.533 --> 00:11:09.767
However, looking back,
I see that I learned it through music,

00:11:09.767 --> 00:11:11.533
which I found interesting
and enjoyed it.

00:11:11.533 --> 00:11:13.200
With Harry Potter books,

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:15.467
that I didn't want to wait for
for the translation to come out.

00:11:15.467 --> 00:11:16.667
Things like that.

00:11:16.667 --> 00:11:19.833
So those grammar lessons in school
weren't the cause.

00:11:19.833 --> 00:11:21.933
And then you think about it and you say,

00:11:21.933 --> 00:11:26.867
"Wow, so many years studying in
school and people do not speak English".

00:11:26.867 --> 00:11:29.100
Many people in Spain do not speak English.

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:30.400
And it's a bit sad, isn't it?

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:32.067
Because so many hours dedicated,

00:11:32.067 --> 00:11:34.300
and if it were done
in a slightly different way

00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:36.467
then perhaps people would talk more.

00:11:36.467 --> 00:11:39.233
Steve: Tell me, I have the impression,

00:11:39.233 --> 00:11:45.167
that the Portuguese speak
English better than the Spanish.

00:11:45.167 --> 00:11:47.500
Do you agree?

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:49.467
Laura: Yes, I have
that impression too.

00:11:49.467 --> 00:11:53.900
Well, that was my impression
when I went to Portugal on a trip.

00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:57.633
I realized that of course,
they speak Portuguese, Spanish and English.

00:11:57.633 --> 00:12:02.267
Of course, also in more touristic areas.

00:12:02.267 --> 00:12:05.667
But I think that in
Spanish in general,

00:12:05.667 --> 00:12:09.433
people think that because it is
a language with so many speakers,

00:12:09.433 --> 00:12:11.067
you just simply "don´t need" English.

00:12:11.067 --> 00:12:15.333
If you look at smaller countries,
for example in Sweden,

00:12:15.333 --> 00:12:16.900
people speak English really well.

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:20.367
In Germany, too, in general.

00:12:20.367 --> 00:12:23.933
Steve: The Portugal and Spain case,
in my opinion, is highly intriguing.

00:12:23.933 --> 00:12:29.100
It is the fact that well,
Spain is bigger than Portugal.

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:33.033
There are more speakers of
Spanish than Portuguese.

00:12:33.033 --> 00:12:36.033
Although in Brazil there are
about 200,000,000.

00:12:36.033 --> 00:12:40.433
Additionally, it seems to me
that there are more sounds, right?

00:12:40.433 --> 00:12:42.300
In Portuguese than in Spanish.

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:43.933
The vowels.

00:12:43.933 --> 00:12:47.867
There is more vowel variety
in Portuguese than in Spanish.

00:12:47.867 --> 00:12:50.400
But also the fact
that in Portugal,

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:54.500
television programs are in their
original version.

00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:56.800
Which is not the case in Spain.

00:12:56.800 --> 00:12:58.267
Laura: Oh, that helps.

00:12:58.267 --> 00:13:00.367
Steve: And in Sweden or in Holland,

00:13:00.367 --> 00:13:05.267
all TV programs are in English,
French, German, etc.

00:13:05.267 --> 00:13:08.833
But not in Spain. They are dubbed.
I think they are dubbed.

00:13:08.833 --> 00:13:11.167
Laura: Oh, yes.
That's correct.

00:13:11.167 --> 00:13:13.167
And then I also have friends who

00:13:13.167 --> 00:13:14.767
sometimes want to practice English

00:13:14.767 --> 00:13:16.367
and put on TV,
for example "Friends",

00:13:16.367 --> 00:13:19.000
but they played it with
Spanish subtitles.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:22.433
And I'm thinking "No, put it on
with English subtitles".

00:13:22.433 --> 00:13:25.767
Because the brain, I think,
always chooses the easiest option.

00:13:25.767 --> 00:13:27.967
Which, in that case, is reading in Spanish.

00:13:27.967 --> 00:13:30.133
So that's what I say.

00:13:30.133 --> 00:13:33.333
What you were saying about vowels,
which is interesting

00:13:33.333 --> 00:13:34.933
because there are 8 in Catalan,
for example.

00:13:34.933 --> 00:13:37.100
Then that also...

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:41.467
And well, the fact of growing up
bilingual even though they are

00:13:41.467 --> 00:13:42.667
similar languages, también ayuda.

00:13:42.667 --> 00:13:43.800
I believe.

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:45.733
Steve: I believe that in
the case of Catalans,

00:13:45.733 --> 00:13:52.300
who´ve learned Catalan and Spanish
since childhood,

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:57.200
this should increase their brain's
adaptability.

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:58.733
Initially.

00:13:58.733 --> 00:14:02.509
So perhaps the Catalans
speak other languages better

00:14:02.867 --> 00:14:04.733
than someone from Madrid.

00:14:05.633 --> 00:14:09.467
Laura: Yes, but in this case,
I believe it is even better,

00:14:09.467 --> 00:14:13.033
for instance, if someone speaks
Basque and Spanish.

00:14:13.033 --> 00:14:14.867
Due to languages differences.

00:14:14.867 --> 00:14:19.400
However, a large number of Catalans
also struggle with their English.

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:23.200
That is, like everywhere else.

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:28.400
Steve: Tell me about
grammatical complications.

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:31.467
For example in Spanish or Catalan,

00:14:31.467 --> 00:14:36.167
many people have difficulty
with verbs

00:14:36.167 --> 00:14:39.133
With conjugations.

00:14:39.133 --> 00:14:40.500
Laura: Conjugation.

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:43.167
Steve: But in German,
there is this problem

00:14:43.167 --> 00:14:46.133
of what is called in English
"Declensions".

00:14:46.133 --> 00:14:48.333
Subjunctives that change.

00:14:49.900 --> 00:14:51.833
Laura: Awful.

00:14:51.833 --> 00:14:58.567
Steve: How were you able to overcome
this obstacle by learning German?

00:14:58.567 --> 00:15:01.100
Laura: Well, yes and no.

00:15:01.100 --> 00:15:03.000
Ja und nein

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:04.267
Actually I´m telling you,

00:15:04.267 --> 00:15:05.833
I speak German quite well,

00:15:05.833 --> 00:15:07.633
I would say I have a C1 level.

00:15:07.633 --> 00:15:10.967
But I don't always do it perfectly.

00:15:10.967 --> 00:15:11.967
Sometimes I forget,

00:15:11.967 --> 00:15:13.500
Is it da, die, das?

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:16.633
However, there are guidelines,
and as you mentioned,

00:15:16.633 --> 00:15:19.233
The brain recalls information
if there is a lot of input.

00:15:19.233 --> 00:15:21.967
There are certain structures
that are automated.

00:15:21.967 --> 00:15:24.733
And it is the same as
happens with da, die, das.

00:15:24.733 --> 00:15:27.567
Sometimes you have to pay attention,

00:15:27.567 --> 00:15:31.700
cause I knew a guy who decided
he wasn't going to learn that.

00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:34.233
He said "I will always say da."

00:15:34.233 --> 00:15:37.933
Always da.
And I said okay, fine.

00:15:37.933 --> 00:15:39.100
You will be understood.

00:15:39.100 --> 00:15:42.633
But I think that's a bad strategy.

00:15:42.633 --> 00:15:45.733
Nor should we go to the other
extreme and say

00:15:45.733 --> 00:15:47.833
"I want to say everything perfect".

00:15:47.833 --> 00:15:49.167
Well, it's going to be hard.

00:15:49.167 --> 00:15:52.400
Over a certain age,
that perfection will come.

00:15:52.400 --> 00:15:55.267
I think it is impossible,
or almost impossible.

00:15:55.267 --> 00:15:56.800
In my opinion.

00:15:56.800 --> 00:15:58.133
Steve: Yes, I agree with you.

00:15:58.133 --> 00:16:02.467
Most people want to
communicate clearly.

00:16:02.467 --> 00:16:08.233
They want to speak according to usage.

00:16:08.233 --> 00:16:10.500
Just as native speakers use the language.

00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:11.833
That´s the goal.

00:16:11.833 --> 00:16:14.467
I want to sound more German.

00:16:14.467 --> 00:16:19.567
But if I'm going to be thinking
all the time if it's die, das, da,

00:16:19.567 --> 00:16:22.467
I won't be able to speak.

00:16:22.467 --> 00:16:25.300
So you need to have balance.

00:16:25.300 --> 00:16:28.167
Yes, I want to speak better.

00:16:28.167 --> 00:16:31.100
But I know that by studying the...

00:16:31.100 --> 00:16:33.200
How do you say?

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:34.367
"the tables".

00:16:34.367 --> 00:16:35.667
Laura: Declinations.

00:16:35.667 --> 00:16:37.000
Steve: Declinations.

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:38.300
Laura: Or the gender of words.

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:42.133
Steve: Yes, studying and
trying to learn and memorize.

00:16:42.133 --> 00:16:43.500
It does not work.

00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:47.000
So it's a combination
in which, from time to time

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:51.933
you are going to pay attention
to those endings.

00:16:51.933 --> 00:16:58.000
However, I'm going to read and
listen during the 80% of the time.

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:02.600
I occasionally plan to review
the rules I've already forgotten.

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:05.567
But I can't think about it
while I'm speaking Spanish.

00:17:05.567 --> 00:17:07.247
I'll errs, I know that.
Err

00:17:07.533 --> 00:17:09.200
Errs is on Portuguese.

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:10.800
Laura: It´s normal.

00:17:10.800 --> 00:17:15.000
Steve: If I'm going to think about
whether it's this or that,

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:18.833
I won't be able to talk.
So yes, it's a balance.

00:17:18.833 --> 00:17:21.167
I cannot ignore it,

00:17:21.167 --> 00:17:25.167
"I'm using der.

00:17:25.167 --> 00:17:26.733
That doesn't work either.

00:17:26.733 --> 00:17:29.500
Laura: That was certainly
going to the limit.

00:17:29.500 --> 00:17:32.367
But yes, I do believe
in a middle ground.

00:17:32.367 --> 00:17:34.300
A balance. Do not go crazy.

00:17:34.300 --> 00:17:38.733
I realize it too, as I started
learning Swedish a year ago.

00:17:38.733 --> 00:17:42.400
I have noticed that my German
has gotten a little bit worse.

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:44.733
But hey, I'll take it, you know?

00:17:44.733 --> 00:17:49.100
I prefer to speak more languages
a little worse,

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:52.300
because I might as well say:

00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:55.000
"I´m focusing only on German,
I'm going to do a C2".

00:17:55.000 --> 00:17:58.267
But that doesn't make me
personally fulfilled.

00:17:58.267 --> 00:18:01.500
Everyone has their own
goals and that is not mine.

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:04.700
So I'm happy, satisfied with
my level and that's it.

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:07.200
Steve: I completely agree with you.

00:18:08.767 --> 00:18:11.733
Understanding is the most
important thing of all

00:18:13.067 --> 00:18:15.300
The most important factor for me

00:18:15.300 --> 00:18:19.167
will be how many languages
I can comprehend and understand

00:18:19.167 --> 00:18:24.500
Sometimes I make more mistakes
than other times when I speak.

00:18:24.500 --> 00:18:29.200
Because now you are studying Swedish,
having a little bit more mistakes in German.

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:32.067
If you focus on German,

00:18:32.067 --> 00:18:35.567
you will speak with fewer
mistakes in German but more in Swedish.

00:18:35.567 --> 00:18:39.000
And what is the importance
of all that?

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:41.333
Laura: None.

00:18:41.333 --> 00:18:42.933
Steve: Exactly, none.

00:18:42.933 --> 00:18:45.900
Is Swedish easier than German?

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:48.100
Laura: Well, I´m not sure.

00:18:48.100 --> 00:18:49.833
Mmmm, yes.

00:18:49.833 --> 00:18:51.067
I think it has helped me a lot

00:18:51.067 --> 00:18:53.133
speak German quite well.

00:18:53.133 --> 00:18:58.300
And then I'll start podcasting
and so on.

00:18:58.300 --> 00:19:00.667
I mean, that motivated me a lot.

00:19:00.667 --> 00:19:04.033
What I don't like is that phase
of not understanding anything.

00:19:04.033 --> 00:19:07.300
I like to have
interesting resources.

00:19:07.300 --> 00:19:10.300
I don't want to study with a
"Hi, my name is x".

00:19:10.300 --> 00:19:11.567
It bores me to death.

00:19:11.567 --> 00:19:15.067
So I spent about
a month with Duolingo,

00:19:15.067 --> 00:19:16.900
a bit with a book I had.

00:19:16.900 --> 00:19:19.867
And then I put on easy podcasts
of A1 or so,

00:19:19.867 --> 00:19:24.100
and now I'm listening to some A2-B1
podcasts and I understand it all.

00:19:24.100 --> 00:19:26.667
Then talk, of course...

00:19:26.667 --> 00:19:27.733
it's a little more difficult for me.

00:19:27.733 --> 00:19:31.100
Because it's all input
and then the output comes out

00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:34.100
but sometimes it works out
well and sometimes not so well.

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:36.100
But that's it.

00:19:36.100 --> 00:19:39.700
Steve: Don't tell me you
didn't use LingQ for Swedish.

00:19:39.700 --> 00:19:41.433
Laura: Yes, I have used it.

00:19:41.433 --> 00:19:43.200
Steve: The mini
stories to start with.

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:49.167
I always use mini stories
to start every language.

00:19:50.633 --> 00:19:55.033
Laura: Yes, I'm actually thinking
of doing an experiment with LingQ.

00:19:55.033 --> 00:19:57.467
Perhaps to learn a new language.

00:19:57.467 --> 00:19:58.800
Steve: Okay, very well.

00:19:58.800 --> 00:20:00.667
Laura: It´s just that I want
to invest time in it.

00:20:00.667 --> 00:20:02.167
That's a bit of the problem.

00:20:02.167 --> 00:20:04.767
The podcasts work very well for me
because, for example,

00:20:04.767 --> 00:20:07.900
I put on a podcast and start
preparing my breakfast.

00:20:07.900 --> 00:20:10.100
I'm in the car,
I put on a podcast.

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:13.933
That fits my life quite well.

00:20:13.933 --> 00:20:16.100
Steve: Let's finish.

00:20:16.100 --> 00:20:20.400
It seems to me that
there are people who think

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:23.567
hat they need a talent
to learn languages.

00:20:23.567 --> 00:20:27.200
In the end, we just need enthusiasm.

00:20:27.200 --> 00:20:31.500
And what it feels to talk with you,
is enthusiasm.

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:33.400
Laura: Oh, thank you.

00:20:33.400 --> 00:20:40.933
Steve: With enthusiasm we can
all learn any language,

00:20:40.933 --> 00:20:43.900
or any number of languages
we want.

00:20:43.900 --> 00:20:47.000
Laura: Yes, I believe
it's really...

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:47.967
Sorry.

00:20:47.967 --> 00:20:51.233
I think it is
enthusiasm and dedication.

00:20:51.233 --> 00:20:53.800
What fascinates me about the book,

00:20:53.800 --> 00:20:57.867
I don't know if you know the book
"Atomic Habits"

00:20:57.867 --> 00:21:01.333
It is a book that I loved
and in fact,

00:21:01.333 --> 00:21:03.533
after reading that book
I started with my channel,

00:21:03.533 --> 00:21:05.767
I started learning Italian, etc.

00:21:05.767 --> 00:21:07.633
Because with little changes,

00:21:10.767 --> 00:21:13.800
with small steps
you reach big changes.

00:21:13.800 --> 00:21:15.700
And that's what
I do with the Swede.

00:21:15.700 --> 00:21:18.800
10 minutes a day minimum,
is my daily routine.

00:21:18.800 --> 00:21:21.733
So I have that rule
and that helps me a lot.

00:21:21.733 --> 00:21:22.800
Steve: Yes.

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:24.333
Exactly.

00:21:24.333 --> 00:21:28.100
Well now we are going to do
an interview on your channel,

00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:31.567
where we will speak
in several languages.

00:21:31.567 --> 00:21:33.900
Laura: Yes, awesome.

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:34.900
Thanks Steve.

00:21:34.900 --> 00:21:35.700
Steve: Thank you so much.

00:21:35.700 --> 00:21:36.567
Great pleasure.

00:21:36.567 --> 00:21:37.183
Laura: Bye.

00:21:37.183 --> 00:21:38.223
Steve: Bye.

